From: Ilies CHERGUI <ichergui@nvidia.com>
Subject: [PATCH] examples: Resolve holoviz example assets in an installed tree

holoviz_textured_quad and holoviz_cuda_interop_benchmark cannot find nv_logo.png
once installed. parse_resource_path_args() defaults to <exedir>/<example>_assets,
a directory only the POST_BUILD command creates, so it exists in the build tree
alone, while install(FILES) places the image directly beside the binary:

  Failed to load image .../holoviz_textured_quad_assets/nv_logo.png: can't fopen

The flat install is deliberate, and matches how the rest of the SDK ships
example data, so resolve the assets rather than move them: try the subdirectory,
then the executable's own directory, taking the first that holds every required
file. A build tree is unaffected, its POST_BUILD copy having populated the first
candidate, which stays the one reported when neither is complete. This
implements the search --help already describes, "the build-tree asset directory
or the installed example directory", of which only the first was ever written.

Upstream-Status: Pending
Signed-off-by: Ilies CHERGUI <ichergui@nvidia.com>
---
diff --git a/modules/visualization/examples/core/example_utils.hpp b/modules/visualization/examples/core/example_utils.hpp
index 9c465d837..3e098cd09 100644
--- a/modules/visualization/examples/core/example_utils.hpp
+++ b/modules/visualization/examples/core/example_utils.hpp
@@ -48,6 +48,22 @@ inline std::filesystem::path current_executable_path(const char* argv0) {
   return std::filesystem::path(argv0);
 }
 
+/// The files every resource path has to provide. Shared by the default-path search and by
+/// the `--print-resource-path` check so the two cannot disagree about what a usable
+/// resource directory is.
+inline constexpr const char* kResourceFileNames[] = {"nv_logo.png"};
+
+/// True when \p dir provides every file in kResourceFileNames.
+inline bool has_resource_files(const std::filesystem::path& dir) {
+  for (const char* file_name : kResourceFileNames) {
+    std::error_code exists_error;
+    if (!std::filesystem::exists(dir / file_name, exists_error)) {
+      return false;
+    }
+  }
+  return true;
+}
+
 /// Result of parse_resource_path_args(): either the resolved resource path, or the exit code
 /// the program should return immediately (after `--help`, `--print-resource-path`, or a usage
 /// error).
@@ -58,7 +74,9 @@ struct ResourcePathArgs {
 
 /// Parse the command line shared by the resource-path examples: one optional positional
 /// resource path plus `--help`/`-h` and `--print-resource-path`. When no path is given,
-/// default to the \p assets_dir_name directory next to the running executable.
+/// look for the assets in the \p assets_dir_name directory next to the running executable,
+/// and failing that beside the executable itself, which is where an installed example
+/// carries them.
 inline ResourcePathArgs parse_resource_path_args(int argc, char** argv,
                                                  const char* assets_dir_name) {
   const auto print_usage = [&](std::ostream& out) {
@@ -93,12 +111,22 @@ inline ResourcePathArgs parse_resource_path_args(int argc, char** argv,
   }
 
   if (res_path.empty()) {
-    res_path = (current_executable_path(argv[0]).parent_path() / assets_dir_name).string();
+    // Two layouts carry the assets. A build tree copies them into a per-example
+    // subdirectory beside the binary, because the core examples all build into one
+    // shared output directory. An installed example has them
+    // directly beside the binary instead: its install destination is already
+    // per-example, so there is nothing for the subdirectory to keep apart. Prefer the
+    // subdirectory, and keep naming it when neither layout is complete, so a build
+    // tree that failed to populate it still reports the file it expected there.
+    const std::filesystem::path exe_dir = current_executable_path(argv[0]).parent_path();
+    const std::filesystem::path assets_dir = exe_dir / assets_dir_name;
+    const bool installed_layout = !has_resource_files(assets_dir) && has_resource_files(exe_dir);
+    res_path = (installed_layout ? exe_dir : assets_dir).string();
   }
 
   if (print_resource_path) {
     const std::filesystem::path assets_path{res_path};
-    for (const char* file_name : {"nv_logo.png"}) {
+    for (const char* file_name : kResourceFileNames) {
       std::error_code exists_error;
       if (!std::filesystem::exists(assets_path / file_name, exists_error)) {
         std::cerr << "Missing " << (assets_path / file_name).string() << "\n";
